r 70s grey mata loss leaves almost  an inch of spare room  that's why elderly people like my  grandfather are so much more prone to  cerebral bleeding after a blow to the  head  the brain actually rattles around inside  and these are just a few of the  descriptions  now you probably are well aware that a  lot of research is being conducted to  try and extend health span  and to prevent aging the period of life  free from disease  but the reality is the number of people  with multiple morbidities is still  expected to increase in the coming years  and along with this lot of function  comes with it loss of independence  and with that as arsenal describes the  experience of advanced aging and death  has shifted to hospitals and nursing  homes and so with loss of independence  during aging comes dependence on others  and who will that be  and so this brings us on to the second  key point i want you to take from this  book  which is who will look after the elderly  now one answer are geriatricians  doctors who are specialized in the  health care of elderly people  however as artel describes the number of  certified geriatricians has fallen in  the u.s by 25  between 1996 and 2010. instead doctors  prefer to specialize in radiology and  plastic surgery  partly because of the low pay associated  with geriatricians  along with the fact that a lot of  doctors don't like looking after elderly  and here azel describes a conversation  with his friend felix silverstone  who was a geriatrician and he says that  with the elderly they don't just have a  chief complaint  they have 15 chief complaints how in the  world are you going to cope with all of  them  you're overwhelmed besides he's had a  number of these things for 50 years or  so  you're not going to cure something he's  had for 50 years he has high blood  pressure  he has diabetes he has arthritis there's  nothing glamorous about taking care of  any of these things  and so at all addresses this problem  well but that doesn't necessarily seem  to be any easy answers  instead he goes on to talk about  alternative options such as nursing  homes and assisted living  i say this now introduces us to dr bill  thomas and  the third taking that i want to talk  about which is regaining independence  and so bill thomas's experience began in  the early 1990s when he got a new job as  medical director of chase memorial  nursing home  in new york and he identifies that there  are three plagues of nursing home  existence  boredom loneliness and helplessness and  so  bill decided that he wanted to do  something about this  so he introduced dogs cats rabbits  hens plants and children all to the  nursing home  and they found in a study that over two  years the number of prescriptions  required per resident fell to half that  of the control nursing home  the total drug costs fell to just 38  of the comparison facility and deaths  fell by 15  so why did that happen well thomas said  i believe the difference in death rates  can be traced to the fundamental human  need for a reason to live  and goes along with thomas's belief that  a good life was one of maximum  independence  which is exactly what his changes were  trying to achieve in place of boredom  they offer spontaneity  in place of loneliness they offer  companionship and in place of  helplessness they offer a chance to take  care of another being  and all of this for thomas was just the  starting point for a larger program  named eden alternative which over the  last  20 or so years has de-institutionalized  nursing homes  and also resulted in the so-called  greenhouse project  and greenhouses not like the grey of  plants  in this respect are small and communal  with a physical environment made to  preserve quality of life  self-sufficiency privacy and dignity and  would host no more than 12 residents  each and if you're like me and like ted  talks then there's  a ted talk given by dr phil thomas on  youtube that i'll link here for you to  check 